WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:15.959
crawled to the hatch and made my way back to the gallery.

2
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.700
The storm was raging, and no one could stand upright.

3
00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:25.242
Finally at midnight Captain Nemo gave the orders to sink below the waves and escape the storm.

4
00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:28.923
We headed northeast, away from the shores of New York.

5
00:00:29.623 --> 00:00:32.684
We were also moving away from Canada, much to Ned's despair.

6
00:00:33.728 --> 00:00:37.291
Like Captain Nemo, he spent most of his time alone during these days.

7
00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:43.476
But Conseil and I stuck together, gazing out the gallery windows and tracing our route on the map

8
00:00:43.537 --> 00:00:44.637
as we crossed the Atlantic.

9
00:00:45.618 --> 00:00:49.482
By May 28 we were within 120 miles of Ireland.

10
00:00:50.262 --> 00:00:52.564
Ned came out of his cabin and peppered me with questions.

11
00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:55.186
Was the submarine headed for Great Britain?

12
00:00:56.107 --> 00:00:59.190
Did I think Captain Nemo would attempt to sail through the English Channel?

13
00:01:00.111 --> 00:01:01.292
Was he taking us near France?

14
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.403
I had no idea what to reply because the captain remained in his cabin.

15
00:01:06.764 --> 00:01:08.745
We could only follow our course on the map.

16
00:01:09.746 --> 00:01:15.191
During the day of May 31, the submarine traveled in circles; it seemed to be seeking a spot

17
00:01:15.231 --> 00:01:16.452
that it could not find.

18
00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:22.837
I was up on the platform at noon when Captain Nemo came to take the submarine's bearings—a task

19
00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:24.618
his lieutenant usually performed.

20
00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:28.922
He did not say a word to me, but he looked gloomier than ever.

21
00:01:29.862 --> 00:01:31.624
What could make him feel so unhappy?

22
00:01:32.416 --> 00:01:34.977
Was it because we were so close to European shores?

23
00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:38.978
Did he have some distressing memories of his abandoned country?

24
00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.319
Was he feeling remorse or regret?

25
00:01:42.279 --> 00:01:47.220
I was haunted by these questions and had a premonition that the captain's secrets would soon be revealed.

26
00:01:48.161 --> 00:01:52.902
The next day, June 1, the Nautilus continued to hunt for something in the water.

27
00:01:53.942 --> 00:01:56.963
Again it was Captain Nemo who came up to the platform at noon.

28
00:01:57.903 --> 00:02:00.084
The sea was beautiful and the sky was clear.

29
00:02:00.936 --> 00:02:05.518
Off to the east I could see a large steamship on the horizon about eight miles away.

30
00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:10.640
No flag fluttered from its mast, so I couldn't tell its country of origin.

31
00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:13.021
The captain took the submarine's bearings.

32
00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:17.083
"It is here," he said as he turned and went below.

33
00:02:18.044 --> 00:02:22.386
Had he seen the steamship, which was now changing course and seemed to be heading for us?

34
00:02:23.166 --> 00:02:23.846
I could not tell.

35
00:02:24.907 --> 00:02:27.548
I returned to the gallery and the Nautilus began to sink.

36
00:02:28.604 --> 00:02:33.867
A few minutes later, it rested on the sea bottom at a depth of 2,500 feet.

37
00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:38.250
I looked through one of the windows and saw nothing except quiet water.

38
00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:43.413
But through the other window I saw a large object encrusted with white shells.

39
00:02:44.473 --> 00:02:48.656
As I looked closer, I realized that it was a ship that must have sunk a long time

40
00:02:48.716 --> 00:02:55.199
ago. "At one time that ship was called the Marseillais, but it was renamed," said Captain Nemo, who

41
00:02:55.219 --> 00:02:56.200
had appeared at my side.

42
00:02:57.264 --> 00:03:04.968
"After a heroic battle, it sank with 356 sailors onboard rather than surrender to the British in 1794.

43
00:03:05.749 --> 00:03:07.530
Today is the anniversary of its sinking."

44
00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.452
My eyes widened as I recalled my French history.

45
00:03:11.472 --> 00:03:14.013
"You mean that's the Avenger?"

46
00:03:14.393 --> 00:03:14.933
I exclaimed.

47
00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:20.737
"Yes, the Avenger," said Captain Nemo, stretching out his hand toward the wreck.

48
00:03:20.757 --> 00:03:22.297
"A good name!"

49
00:03:23.378 --> 00:03:26.940
My eyes did not leave the captain, who was gazing reverently at the wreck.

50
00:03:27.976 --> 00:03:33.138
I might never know Captain Nemo's real name, where he was from, or where he was going.

51
00:03:34.239 --> 00:03:38.360
But I had just seen him moved by this shipwreck, and that gave me a clue to his

52
00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:39.601
character and his past.

53
00:03:40.681 --> 00:03:44.743
It was a strange hatred that had locked Captain Nemo inside the Nautilus with his crew.

54
00:03:45.743 --> 00:03:48.024
And it was a hatred that time could not diminish.

55
00:03:48.825 --> 00:03:50.906
I wondered if he still hungered for revenge.

56
00:03:51.886 --> 00:03:56.288
The Nautilus began to slowly rise to the surface, and the Avenger disappeared from view.

57
00:03:57.372 --> 00:04:01.014
Soon a slight rolling motion told me that we were in the open air.

58
00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:03.715
At that moment I heard a dull boom.

59
00:04:05.235 --> 00:04:07.496
I looked at the captain, but he did not move.

60
00:04:07.516 --> 00:04:09.697
"Captain!" I said.

61
00:04:09.717 --> 00:04:11.498
"What was that sound?"

62
00:04:12.478 --> 00:04:15.739
He did not answer, so I left him and climbed up to the platform.

63
00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.140
Ned and Conseil were already there.

64
00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.401
"Where did that sound come from?"

65
00:04:20.922 --> 00:04:21.302
I asked.

66
00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:24.503
"It was cannon fire," replied Ned.

67
00:04:25.483 --> 00:04:29.322
I looked toward the east, where I had last seen that steamship.

68
00:04:30.142 --> 00:04:34.665
It was headed straight for us and putting on steam, but it was still miles away.

69
00:04:34.685 --> 00:04:37.087
"What is that ship, Ned?"

70
00:04:37.587 --> 00:04:38.008
I asked.

71
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:42.691
"From its rigging and sails, it looks like a warship," said Ned.

72
00:04:42.711 --> 00:04:48.075
"May it reach us, and, if necessary, sink this cursed submarine."

73
00:04:49.156 --> 00:04:50.497
"Now, how will it do that?"

74
00:04:50.977 --> 00:04:51.778
asked Conseil.

75
00:04:51.798 --> 00:04:54.900
"It can't fire at us if we sink beneath the waves."

76
00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.849
I didn't give Ned a chance to respond.

77
00:04:57.869 --> 00:05:01.372
"Can you recognize which country it belongs to?"

78
00:05:01.832 --> 00:05:02.232
I asked.

79
00:05:03.233 --> 00:05:05.915
Ned squinted at the ship in the distance for a few moments.

80
00:05:07.256 --> 00:05:08.457
"No, Professor Aronnax.

81
00:05:08.837 --> 00:05:11.859
I can't tell where it's from because it's not flying any flag.

82
00:05:12.159 --> 00:05:16.302
But it's definitely a warship because there's a long pennant flying from its mainmast."

83
00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:21.065
For a quarter of an hour, we watched the ship rapidly steaming toward us.

84
00:05:22.166 --> 00:05:24.788
Thick black smoke was pouring from its two funnels.

85
00:05:25.844 --> 00:05:30.428
I had a hard time believing that anyone could see the Nautilus from that distance, but the ship

86
00:05:30.468 --> 00:05:31.749
did seem focused on us.

87
00:05:31.769 --> 00:05:38.634
"Professor," said Ned, "if that vessel passes within a mile of us, I'm going to throw myself into

88
00:05:38.674 --> 00:05:41.376
the sea, and I advise you two to do the same.

89
00:05:41.856 --> 00:05:45.118
It will pick us up and we'll be off this wretched submarine for good."

90
00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:50.623
I didn't reply to Ned's suggestion but continued to watch the ship approach.

91
00:05:50.643 --> 00:05:56.809
"Master, you may recall that we have some experience with swimming," said Conseil.

92
00:05:56.829 --> 00:06:03.431
"Yes, Conseil, I remember that—" I stopped speaking as a puff of white smoke burst from the bow

93
00:06:03.451 --> 00:06:03.891
of the ship.

94
00:06:04.911 --> 00:06:10.653
A few seconds later, something heavy made a big splash near the submarine's stern, and I heard a

95
00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:11.593
loud explosion.

96
00:06:11.613 --> 00:06:14.054
"What? They're firing at us!"

97
00:06:14.554 --> 00:06:15.094
I exclaimed.

98
00:06:15.894 --> 00:06:17.835
"Surely they can see that there are men onboard."

99
00:06:17.855 --> 00:06:21.016
"That may be exactly why they're firing on us."

100
00:06:21.456 --> 00:06:22.056
Ned looked at me.

101
00:06:23.124 --> 00:06:25.466
And suddenly I understood what was happening.

102
00:06:26.467 --> 00:06:31.310
I thought back to November when we were hunting the giant narwhal onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

103
00:06:32.771 --> 00:06:38.776
When Ned's harpoon hit the Nautilus, Captain Farragut must have realized that he was dealing with a dangerous

104
00:06:38.836 --> 00:06:40.997
submarine and not a sea creature.

105
00:06:42.038 --> 00:06:44.180
He obviously hadn't kept that news to himself.

106
00:06:45.301 --> 00:06:49.664
Ships had probably been crisscrossing the oceans in search of this underwater vessel ever since.

107
00:06:50.732 --> 00:06:56.195
To my horror, I also realized that Captain Nemo had used the Nautilus as an instrument of revenge.

108
00:06:57.196 --> 00:07:01.638
On the night when he'd locked us in that cell and drugged our food, the submarine must have

109
00:07:01.678 --> 00:07:02.659
attacked another ship.

110
00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:06.841
The man buried in the coral cemetery must have died in that battle.

111
00:07:07.921 --> 00:07:10.483
Someone knew who Captain Nemo really was.

112
00:07:11.683 --> 00:07:17.306
Someone knew that he was out to avenge some horrible wrong, and now whole nations were hunting him

113
00:07:17.346 --> 00:07:19.948
down! I faced the grim truth.

114
00:07:20.776 --> 00:07:25.703
Instead of meeting friends onboard this approaching ship, we could only expect merciless enemies.
